libir - the infrared protocols of the Unleashed firmware (spec Phase 7)
=======================================================================

The stock Flipper Zero infrared encoders and decoders and a reader/writer
for the stock .ir files, for the `infrared` app (flipper/gui/infrared.c)
and the host test (flipper/tests/infrared, `make irtest`).  Issue #389.

Source: DarkFlippers/unleashed-firmware, lib/infrared at commit
53818b18f63e105baaf37ed8eac9a7def58ec8b1 (2026-11-16), GPLv3 (as this
project), the same commit as src/lib/subghz and src/lib/lfrfid.  Copied
byte for byte (checked with cmp against that commit):

  encoder_decoder/*             all of lib/infrared/encoder_decoder: NEC,
                                NECext, NEC42, NEC42ext, Samsung32, RC5,
                                RC5X, RC6, SIRC, SIRC15, SIRC20, Kaseikyo,
                                RCA, Pioneer, the common pulse-distance
                                and Manchester engines, the timing tables

Not taken:

  worker/                       infrared_worker.c (a FuriThread with a
                                stream buffer fed by the HAL's capture
                                interrupt) and infrared_transmit.c (a
                                DMA-fed HAL transmitter).  On MINIX the
                                kernel's /dev/ir does that part: it
                                captures marks and spaces into a ring and
                                plays a list of durations (flipdev.c).
  signal/                       infrared_signal.c and infrared_brute_force.c
                                read with FlipperFormat and keep whole
                                raw lines in FuriStrings and the button
                                index of a database in an m-dict.
                                irfile.c below does both as a stream.

Ours (GPLv3):

  shim/irshim.h, irshim.c       furi_assert/furi_check/furi_crash, COUNT_OF,
                                MIN, MAX and stock's malloc (zeroed, never
                                NULL).  Given to the compiler with
                                -include irshim.h; shim/core/*.h point to it.
  irfile.h, irfile.c            the .ir file formats ("IR signals file",
                                "IR library file", "IR tests file", version
                                1) read one key at a time with getc and
                                written byte for byte as stock writes them.
                                A raw signal's timings go straight into
                                the caller's buffer (stock's limit, 1024);
                                no line and no file is held in memory, so
                                the universal databases are searched
                                signal by signal (irf_find).

RAM (heap, measured by irtest on the 64-bit host, less with the Flipper's
32-bit pointers): all decoders 712 B, the encoder set 16 B (each encoder's
state comes when a message is first encoded).  irfile needs no heap; the
caller gives the timing buffer (4 B a timing).
